From 1f27c296dc3adbac98e194aea41682dc47e8338b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: M2 Gate Closure Audit <audit@local>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 18:15:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] feat(H3.3b): add simulated annealing over swap moves

src/tree_packing/geometry/truncation.py::refine_truncation.

Two anomalies caught by the preceding test commit, in sequence, not
smoothed over:

1. A plain single-tree swap (pick one included tree, one excluded
   candidate, swap, accept if the bounding side shrinks) found zero
   improving moves in 4000 iterations at n=50. Diagnosed directly: the
   tiling is periodic, so whole rows of included trees commonly share the
   exact coordinate defining the current bounding extreme (five separate
   trees tied at each of miny and maxy at n=50). Swapping out any one
   tied member leaves the bound completely unchanged while four others
   still hold it -- the single-swap objective is flat with zero gradient
   near this configuration, so no amount of iteration finds an improving
   move because none exists at that granularity.

2. Fixed by swapping an entire tied boundary group at once -- but the
   first version of this chose replacements by sorting candidates on
   their single-axis contribution to the binding extreme alone (e.g.
   smallest contribution to maxy). That reliably picks candidates with
   very negative y to relieve a high maxy bound, which promptly become
   the new, equally bad miny record instead of relieving anything. Fixed
   by evaluating candidates against the actual resulting bounding side
   (temporarily substitute, measure, revert) for each vacancy in a capped
   nearest-candidate frontier, rather than a single-axis proxy.

Green: pytest tests/unit/test_truncation_refinement.py -- 3 passed.
Independently cross-checked against reference/evaluator.py::check_overlap
for n = 21, 50, 100, 150, 200: zero overlaps, exactly n unique trees at
every sampled n.

Measured: real, meaningful improvement at every sampled n (roughly a
third-to-half reduction in loss vs. the H3.3 baseline), but not yet at the
milestone doc's own worked-example numbers (27.4% vs. its 18% at n=50;
16.4% vs. its 6% at n=200) -- recorded as-is rather than rounded up.
Likely headroom remains in iteration count, frontier size, and
multi-restart seeding, none tuned yet.

Full Gate green: ruff format/check, mypy (31 files), lint-imports, pytest.
